---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import FAQSection from '../../components/FAQSection.astro';
import Screenshot from '../../components/Screenshot.astro';
import DemoVideo from '../../components/DemoVideo.astro';
import { config } from '../../config';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is a construction CRM?',
    answer:
      'A construction CRM is software for tracking the people you sell to and the jobs you are chasing — not a generic contact database. For specialty subcontractors that means GC, owner, and vendor contacts plus a sales pipeline of leads with stage, dollar value, and expected close date. In BuildWorkPro, a won lead converts into a bid or project so the same record runs from first invite to pay app.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is construction lead software?',
    answer:
      'Construction lead software tracks every opportunity before it becomes a bid: who sent the invite, what it is worth, which stage it sits in, and what follow-up is due. BuildWorkPro is construction lead software for specialty subcontractors — each job you are chasing is a lead on a Kanban pipeline with value, probability, and an activity log of calls, emails, and site visits. When the lead lands, it becomes the bid without retyping.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a construction sales CRM?',
    answer:
      'A construction sales CRM is CRM built around how subcontractors sell work: GC and owner contacts, pipeline stages like Site Visit Scheduled and Bid Sent, and a path from the won lead into estimating and billing. Generic sales CRMs stop at the deal. BuildWorkPro carries the same contact from first call through the bid, the project, and the pay application.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does the sales pipeline work?',
    answer:
      'Leads appear as cards on a Kanban board, organized into stage columns. A new workspace starts with seven stages — New Lead, Contacted, Site Visit Scheduled, Bid Sent, Negotiation, Won, and Lost — and you can add, rename, reorder, and recolor stages to match how you actually sell. Drag a card to move it to another stage, or switch to the Cards or Table view of the same pipeline.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does a lead connect to a bid?',
    answer:
      'Yes. From the lead detail page, Create Bid starts a bid pre-linked to the lead, carrying its contact and details over — no retyping. You can also convert a lead straight into a project, which moves the lead to the Won stage.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does contact management work?',
    answer:
      'Every contact gets a type — Customer, General Contractor, Architect, City, HOA, Vendor, Subcontractor, Employee, or Other — so you can filter the list to exactly who you need. Contacts can be hierarchical: set a parent company to nest a person under the company they work for. You can also bulk-import contacts from CSV with column mapping.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why use a CRM built into the same tool as bidding?',
    answer:
      "A standalone CRM stops at the won deal — then you re-enter everything into whatever you estimate and invoice with. Because BuildWorkPro's CRM lives in the same system as bidding and projects, the lead you win becomes the bid, the bid becomes the project, and the contact behind it stays attached the whole way. One record from first call to final pay app.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Which trades is the CRM built for?',
    answer:
      'Trade subcontractors who sell work to GCs and property owners: drywall and sheetrock crews, plumbing, electrical, HVAC, glass and glazing, concrete, framing, roofing, flooring, painting, and excavation contractors all run the same motion — chase the job, bid it, win it, bill it. The pipeline stages are fully editable, so the board matches how your trade actually sells.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does a construction CRM cost?',
    answer:
      'The CRM is included in the single BuildWorkPro plan: $79/month (or $790/year) with unlimited users and every feature — bidding, projects, pay applications, and more. There is a 14-day free trial with no credit card required.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Construction Sales CRM & Lead Software | BuildWorkPro"
  description="Construction lead software and sales CRM for specialty subcontractors. Kanban pipeline, follow-ups, and lead-to-bid conversion. $79/month, 14-day free trial."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-16">
      <div class="text-center">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Features</p>
        <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">
          Construction CRM & Lead Software for Subcontractors
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-slate-500 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Construction sales CRM for specialty subcontractors — track every lead from first GC
          invite to won job on a drag-and-drop pipeline, then start the bid from the same record
          instead of retyping it.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-4">
          <a
            href={config.signupUrl}
            class="inline-flex items-center px-8 py-3.5 text-base font-semibold text-white bg-brand-600 hover:bg-brand-700 rounded-xl transition-all hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg"
          >
            Start free trial
          </a>
          <a
            href="/features/"
            class="inline-flex items-center px-8 py-3.5 text-base font-semibold text-slate-700 bg-white hover:bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl transition-all"
          >
            All features
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Product proof -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <DemoVideo
        src="/videos/pipeline-demo.mp4"
        poster="/videos/pipeline-demo-poster.jpg"
        label="Demo: dragging a lead from New Lead to Contacted on the sales pipeline, then opening it"
        caption="The sales pipeline in BuildWorkPro: drag a lead to its next stage and both column totals update — open it and the stage change is already in its activity."
      />
    </section>

    <!-- Overview -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-slate mx-auto">
        <h2>Get your pipeline out of your inbox</h2>
        <p>
          If your pipeline is a notepad, a whiteboard, and a pile of plan invites in your inbox, you
          don't actually know what's in play — what's waiting on a callback, what needs a site
          visit, what went quiet after you sent the number. BuildWorkPro is construction lead
          software that puts every opportunity on a
          <a href="/docs/crm/pipeline/">sales pipeline</a>: each lead is a card, each column is a
          stage, and dragging a card is how the deal moves from New Lead to Contacted to Bid Sent to
          Won.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each lead tracks a pipeline value, a probability, and an expected close date, and its
          summary card shows the weighted value — pipeline value times probability — so a long shot
          doesn't read like sure money. Calls, emails, meetings, and notes get
          <a href="/docs/crm/leads/">logged against the lead</a> on its timeline. When the deal advances,
          one click starts a
          <a href="/features/construction-bidding/">construction bid</a> pre-linked to the lead, with
          its contact and details carried over. The same contact stays attached through the project and
          the first
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay application</a>. After you win, the same project
          record is where you write
          <a href="/features/change-orders/">construction change orders</a>
          and bill them — no second system.
        </p>
        <p>
          Underneath it all is a <a href="/docs/crm/contacts/">contact list</a> built for construction:
          every contact is typed — Customer, General Contractor, Architect, Vendor, and more — and people
          can nest under a parent company. New to running a pipeline? Start with our
          <a href="/blog/construction-crm-tips/">construction CRM tips</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Construction sales CRM workflow -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-slate mx-auto">
        <h2>The construction sales CRM workflow</h2>
        <p>
          Generic CRMs track “deals.” Specialty subcontractors chase invitations, site visits, and
          bid due dates. Construction sales software has to match that motion: capture the lead,
          qualify it, work it after the number goes out, then convert the win without a second
          system.
        </p>
        <h3>Capture every invite as a lead</h3>
        <p>
          Plan invites, GC emails, and walk-throughs land as leads on the board — named, linked to a
          contact, and tagged with a source and project type. Pipeline value and expected close date
          sit on the card so you can see what is actually in play this week, not just what arrived
          in your inbox.
        </p>
        <h3>Qualify before you estimate</h3>
        <p>
          Not every invite is worth a takeoff. Move a lead through Contacted and Site Visit
          Scheduled while you log the calls and the walk-through. Probability and weighted value
          keep a long-shot from crowding out the jobs you can win. Stages are editable, so the
          columns match how your trade actually sells.
        </p>
        <h3>Follow up after Bid Sent</h3>
        <p>
          Once the estimate is out, the lead stays on the pipeline in Bid Sent or Negotiation. Log
          the follow-up, the addendum, the “did you get our number?” call — the timeline is the
          record, not a thread you have to hunt. When the GC awards the work, Create Bid (if you
          have not already) or Convert to Project takes the same contact into execution and the
          first
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay application</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- vs GC-first CRM -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-4">
        Not a GC-first CRM
      </h2>
      <p class="text-slate-500 text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto mb-10 leading-relaxed">
        Most construction CRMs are built for general contractors who buy work. Specialty
        subcontractors sell work to those GCs — so construction lead software has to run a sales
        pipeline, not a bid-leveling inbox.
      </p>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
            GC-first tools
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-4">Buy-side coordination</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
            <li>Built to collect and compare bids from many trades, not to chase your next job.</li>
            <li>
              Contacts are owners and architects on the GC's project — not the GCs you sell to.
            </li>
            <li>
              Pipeline stages look like generic Prospect / Qualified, not Bid Sent after a takeoff.
            </li>
            <li>
              A won “deal” does not become <em>your</em> estimate, project, or pay app.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            Fine when a GC requires collaborator access on their job. That access is not a sales CRM
            for your company — see
            <a
              href="/blog/subcontractor-vs-general-contractor-software/"
              class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
              >subcontractor vs. general contractor software</a
            >. Volume-quoted enterprise CRM is a different cost model; independently reported
            <a
              href="/blog/procore-pricing-for-subcontractors/"
              class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Procore pricing for subcontractors</a
            >
            scales with construction volume.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-brand-200 bg-brand-50/40 ring-1 ring-brand-100">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
            Subcontractor sales CRM
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-4">
            BuildWorkPro construction lead software
          </h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-sm text-slate-600">
            <li>GC, owner, and vendor contacts typed for how specialty trades actually sell.</li>
            <li>
              A Kanban lead pipeline with stages like Site Visit Scheduled and Bid Sent — editable
              to match your trade.
            </li>
            <li>
              Activity, probability, and weighted pipeline value on every opportunity you are
              chasing.
            </li>
            <li>
              A won lead becomes a
              <a href="/features/construction-bidding/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
                >construction bid</a
              >, then a project and
              <a href="/features/pay-applications/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
                >pay application</a
              >.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-500">
            $79/month flat, unlimited users, 14-day free trial. CRM, bidding, and billing are one
            plan — not a separate sales SKU.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Key Capabilities -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-12">
        Key capabilities
      </h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 ring-1 ring-emerald-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M15 19.128a9.38 9.38 0 002.625.372 9.337 9.337 0 004.121-.952 4.125 4.125 0 00-7.533-2.493M15 19.128v-.003c0-1.113-.285-2.16-.786-3.07M15 19.128v.106A12.318 12.318 0 018.624 21c-2.331 0-4.512-.645-6.374-1.766l-.001-.109a6.375 6.375 0 0111.964-3.07M12 6.375a3.375 3.375 0 11-6.75 0 3.375 3.375 0 016.75 0zm8.25 2.25a2.625 2.625 0 11-5.25 0 2.625 2.625 0 015.25 0z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Contact management</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            One list for everyone you work with, typed as Customer, General Contractor, Architect,
            City, HOA, Vendor, Subcontractor, Employee, or Other — filter to exactly who you need,
            and nest people under a parent company.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 ring-1 ring-emerald-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M9 4.5v15m6-15v15m-10.875 0h15.75c.621 0 1.125-.504 1.125-1.125V5.625c0-.621-.504-1.125-1.125-1.125H4.125c-.621 0-1.125.504-1.125 1.125v12.75c0 .621.504 1.125 1.125 1.125z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Construction sales pipeline</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Leads as cards in stage columns — drag a card to move the deal forward. Prefer a
            different layout? The same pipeline also comes as a Cards grid and a row-per-lead Table.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 ring-1 ring-emerald-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M10.5 6h9.75M10.5 6a1.5 1.5 0 11-3 0m3 0a1.5 1.5 0 10-3 0M3.75 6H7.5m3 12h9.75m-9.75 0a1.5 1.5 0 01-3 0m3 0a1.5 1.5 0 00-3 0m-3.75 0H7.5m9-6h3.75m-3.75 0a1.5 1.5 0 01-3 0m3 0a1.5 1.5 0 00-3 0m-9.75 0h9.75"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Configurable stages</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Start with seven default stages — New Lead, Contacted, Site Visit Scheduled, Bid Sent,
            Negotiation, Won, Lost — then add, rename, reorder, and recolor them to match how you
            actually sell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 ring-1 ring-emerald-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M12 6v12m-3-2.818l.879.659c1.171.879 3.07.879 4.242 0 1.172-.879 1.172-2.303 0-3.182C13.536 12.219 12.768 12 12 12c-.725 0-1.45-.22-2.003-.659-1.106-.879-1.106-2.303 0-3.182s2.9-.879 4.006 0l.415.33M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Weighted pipeline value</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Every lead carries a pipeline value, probability, and expected close date. Its summary
            card shows the weighted value — value times probability — so you see realistic numbers
            instead of wishful ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 ring-1 ring-emerald-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M8.625 12a.375.375 0 11-.75 0 .375.375 0 01.75 0zm0 0H8.25m4.125 0a.375.375 0 11-.75 0 .375.375 0 01.75 0zm0 0H12m4.125 0a.375.375 0 11-.75 0 .375.375 0 01.75 0zm0 0h-.375M21 12c0 4.556-4.03 8.25-9 8.25a9.764 9.764 0 01-2.555-.337A5.972 5.972 0 015.41 20.97a5.969 5.969 0 01-.474-.065 4.48 4.48 0 00.978-2.025c.09-.457-.133-.901-.467-1.226C3.93 16.178 3 14.189 3 12c0-4.556 4.03-8.25 9-8.25s9 3.694 9 8.25z"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Activity timeline</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            Log phone calls, emails, meetings, and notes against a lead. Everything lands on the
            lead's timeline, so you can see where the conversation stands — and logged activity
            feeds the pipeline reports.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-100 bg-white">
          <div class="inline-flex p-3 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 ring-1 ring-emerald-100 mb-4">
            <svg
              class="w-6 h-6 text-emerald-600"
              fill="none"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.5"
            >
              <path
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                d="M16.023 9.348h4.992v-.001M2.985 19.644v-4.992m0 0h4.992m-4.993 0l3.181 3.183a8.25 8.25 0 0013.803-3.7M4.031 9.865a8.25 8.25 0 0113.803-3.7l3.181 3.182"
              ></path>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">Lead-to-bid conversion</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
            When a lead advances, start a
            <a href="/features/construction-bidding/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
              >construction bid</a
            > pre-linked to it — contact and details carry over. Won without a formal bid? Convert the
            lead straight into a project.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Lead detail screenshot -->
    <section class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <Screenshot
        src="/screenshots/27-lead-detail.png"
        alt="BuildWorkPro lead detail page showing pipeline value, probability, weighted value, and activity"
        caption="A lead's detail page: pipeline value, probability, and expected close date, with the weighted value and related activity alongside."
      />
    </section>

    <!-- How It Works -->
    <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mb-20">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 text-center mb-12">How it works</h2>
      <div class="space-y-8">
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            1
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Build your contact list</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Add GCs, customers, and vendors one at a time, or bulk-import from CSV with column
              mapping. Every contact gets a type and at least one way to reach them — email, phone,
              or mobile.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            2
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Add a lead</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Name the opportunity, link a contact, and set the pipeline value, source, and project
              type. The lead starts in your default stage.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            3
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Work the board</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Drag leads across stages as deals move. Log calls, emails, meetings, and notes on each
              lead, and keep the probability and expected close date honest as you learn more.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4">
          <div
            class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold"
          >
            4
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-1">Convert the win</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-500">
              Click Create Bid to start a bid pre-linked to the lead, or Convert to Project to take
              it straight to execution — the lead moves to the Won stage. The same contact carries
              into
              <a href="/features/pay-applications/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
                >pay applications</a
              >
              once the job is underway.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-10 text-center text-sm text-slate-400">
        Full walkthrough in the docs:
        <a href="/docs/crm/pipeline/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Sales pipeline</a>
        ·
        <a href="/docs/crm/leads/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Leads</a>
        ·
        <a href="/docs/crm/contacts/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">Contacts</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <FAQSection faqs={faqs} />

    <!-- Bottom CTA -->
    <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-4">
        Run your construction sales pipeline from a board, not a notepad
      </h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-500 mb-8">
        Construction lead software is included in the $79/month flat plan — unlimited users, every
        feature. 14-day free trial, no credit card required.
      </p>
      <a
        href={config.signupUrl}
